1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,903
The original jack-o'-lantern wasn't a pumpkin.

2
00:00:02,903 --> 00:00:04,964
It was a turnip, and it was terrifying.

3
00:00:04,964 --> 00:00:11,729
Over 2,000 years ago in Ireland, during a festival called Samhain, people believed spirits walked the Earth.

4
00:00:11,729 --> 00:00:15,962
So they carved creepy faces into turnips and potatoes to scare them away.

5
00:00:15,962 --> 00:00:20,826
The name comes from an Irish legend about "Stingy Jack," a man who tricked the devil.

6
00:00:20,826 --> 00:00:25,049
He was doomed to wander the night with only a coal glowing inside a carved turnip.

7
00:00:25,049 --> 00:00:27,743
But here's the part most Americans never learned...

8
00:00:27,743 --> 00:00:34,881
In the 1800s, Irish immigrants arrived in America and found pumpkins: bigger, softer, and way easier to carve.

9
00:00:34,881 --> 00:00:36,530
The turnip never stood a chance.

10
00:00:36,530 --> 00:00:39,830
Subscribe, or Stingy Jack haunts your Wi-Fi.
